<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · War and march · 1868 · Americas</p><h1 class="sg-title">La Bayamesa</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="es">La Bayamesa (Himno de Bayamo)</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>Cuba's national anthem, written on horseback in the town of Bayamo in 1868 during the fight for independence from Spain.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Perucho Figueredo</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Perucho Figueredo; Antonio Rodríguez Ferrer</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Cuban independence fighters; later the Republic of Cuba</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Bayamo, Cuba</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>20 October 1868</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem · War and march</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Perucho Figueredo (1868; he was executed two years later))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-bayamesa">La Bayamesa</a>&quot;, officially the &quot;Himno de Bayamo&quot; (Bayamo Anthem), was first performed in 1868 during the Battle of Bayamo. On 20 October 1868 Cuban forces obtained the surrender of the Spanish colonial authorities in Bayamo, and the jubilant townspeople surrounded Perucho Figueredo, who had taken part in the battle, and asked him to write an anthem to the melody they were humming. He wrote the words there and then, in the saddle of his horse; the original was longer than the official version of today. He composed the melody together with Antonio Rodríguez Ferrer, who contributed its opening notes.</p><p>Two years later Figueredo was captured by the Spanish and executed by firing squad. Just before the order to fire, he shouted a line of his own song.</p><p>The song originally had three verses. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> adopted it as its national anthem in 1902, on the foundation of the Republic, the last two were left out, because they were seen as excessively anti-Spanish and too long compared with the first. The anthem was kept after the revolution of 1959.</p><p>Two other well-known Cuban songs share the name. The first &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-bayamesa">La Bayamesa</a>&quot; was composed in 1851 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlos-manuel-de-cespedes">Carlos Manuel de Céspedes</a> and José Fornaris at the request of their friend Francisco Castillo Moreno, who is sometimes also credited with its words; after 1868, during the Cuban war, a &quot;mambí&quot; version with the same melody and different words became popular. In 1918 the composer and trovador Sindo Garay of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> wrote &quot;Mujer Bayamesa&quot;, which popular use shortened to &quot;La Bayamesa&quot;.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A call to the people of Bayamo and of all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> to take up arms for the homeland and throw off Spanish rule, holding that to die for one's country is an honour and no cause for fear. The mood is urgent and martial.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Spanish</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Spanish</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Al combate corred Bayameses,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To combat run, men of Bayamo,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">que la patria os contempla orgullosa,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>for the homeland looks on you with pride;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">No temais una muerte gloriosa</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>do not fear a glorious death,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">que morir por la patria es vivir!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>for to die for the homeland is to live!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">En cadenas vivir, es vivir</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To live in chains is to live</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">en afrenta y oprobio sumido.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>sunk in affront and disgrace.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Del clarín escuchad el sonido.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Hear the sound of the bugle.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">¡á las armas valientes corred!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To arms, brave ones, run!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Del clarín escuchad el sonido.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Hear the sound of the bugle.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">¡á las armas valientes corred!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To arms, brave ones, run!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">No temais los feroces Iberos</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Do not fear the fierce Iberians:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Son cobardes cual todo tirano</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>they are cowards, like every tyrant;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">no resisten al brazo Cubano</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>they cannot withstand the Cuban arm;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">para siempre su imperio calló.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>their empire has fallen for ever.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">¡Cuba libre! ya España murió</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Free Cuba! Spain has died now;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">su poder y su orgullo ¿do es ido?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>its power and its pride, where have they gone?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">¡del clarín escuchad el sonido</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Hear the sound of the bugle,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">¡¡a las armas!! valientes, corred!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>to arms! brave ones, run!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Contemplad nuestras huestes triunfantes</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Behold our triumphant hosts,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">contempladlos a ellos caídos,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>behold them, fallen,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">por cobardes huyeron vencidos:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>as cowards they fled, defeated:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">por valientes sabemos triunfar!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>as brave men we know how to triumph!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">¡Cuba libre! podemos gritar</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Free Cuba! we can cry</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">del cañón al terrible estampido,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>at the terrible blast of the cannon;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">¡del clarín escuchad el sonido,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>hear the sound of the bugle,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">¡¡á las armas!! valientes, corred!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>to arms! brave ones, run!</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Perucho Figueredo (1868). Text from <a href="https://es.wikisource.org/wiki/La_Bayamesa_(1868)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (4)</summary><ul><li>line 1.1: 'Bayameses': the people of Bayamo, the town in eastern Cuba where the song was first sung (1868).</li><li>lines 2.4, 4.4, 6.4: 'valientes' is read as an address ('brave ones'); it can also be taken as 'run bravely'.</li><li>line 3.4: the text has 'calló' ('fell silent'), here read as 'cayó' ('fell'), which the sense requires; literally 'their empire has fallen silent for ever'.</li><li>line 4.2: '¿do es ido?' is an archaic 'where has it gone?'.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
